# Gitleaks findings that were read and are not secrets. One fingerprint per line,
# written exactly as Gitleaks prints it: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>. A fingerprint
# names one commit, one file and one line, so it silences exactly that finding and
# nothing that resembles it (unlike a path or regex allowlist in .gitleaks.toml).
#
# All three are in commit 3d44462e (2026-11-13), the only commit of the branch
# fix/simpelv1-template-javascript (closed PR #1100, superseded by the removal of v1
# in #1109), so they were never on main. The scheduled scan reads every branch and the
# whole history (push and pull-request scans read only the new commits), which is why
# it failed every night while CI stayed green. Deleting that stale branch would also
# make the findings disappear; this file silences them without deleting anything.
#
# dropzone: `this.KEY_STR` is the public Base64 alphabet (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, "+",
# "/", "=") used by its image-orientation code. The literal is NOT repeated here
# on purpose: gitleaks scans this file too, and the alphabet itself matches
# `generic-api-key` — writing it out silences three findings and adds one.
